  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nExpansive upper low continues to churn across the panhandles this \nafternoon, continuing the abundant moisture transport throughout the \nsouthern and eastern half of the central plains. Towards the sfc a \nslow moving boundary has been the focus for training convection this \nafternoon, oriented southwest to northeast over northeast Kansas. As \nthe boundary slowly sags southward into east central Kansas, a stout \nlow level jet and MUCAPE up to 1000 J/KG provides ample forcing for \nwidespread showers and scattered storms redeveloping and slowly \nmoving along the front during the evening period. Rainfall rates \nwithin the heavier storms range from 1 to 2 inches per hour, and \ngive that some locations have already received 0.5 to 2 inches thus \nfar, it seemed conceivable that flash flooding may become a threat \nfor far eastern Kansas, therefore expanded the Flood Watch to \ninclude those areas this evening. Forecast total rainfall amounts \nare generally coming into better agreement among the REFS and HRRR \nmodels from 1 to 3 inches, up to 6 inches or more in a few locations.\n\nThe rain is expected to exit eastward early Friday morning as much a \nmuch drier airmass overspreads the region behind a cold front. \nNortheast winds increase late this evening from 15 to 25 mph, \noccasionally gusting to 40 mph through the afternoon. Highs Friday \ninto the weekend are in the upper 60s to low 70s while dewpoints are \nmuch more pleasant in the 40s and 50s. Upper ridge amplifies to the \nwest this weekend, spreading east into the plains next week. \nTemperatures moderate some close to 80 degrees for highs on \nWednesday, but overall stays dry and seasonable for this time of \nyear.",
